  "title": "How to store a doll (and look after her skeleton) so she stays in great condition",
  "description": "The best ways to store a TPE or silicone doll: hanging, lying flat, cases and head stands. Plus how to protect the skeleton and avoid flat spots, stains and tears.",
  "category": "Care",
  "readMins": 7,
  "answer": "The best way to store a full-size doll is hanging upright from a proper neck or shoulder hanging kit, completely dry, lightly powdered (if TPE), wearing light-coloured clothing or none, and somewhere cool, dark and dry. If you can't hang her, lay her on her back on a soft, padded surface and change her position regularly. Never fold her, leave her in extreme poses or store her touching dark fabrics or another doll.",
  "faqs": [
   [
    "Is it better to hang a doll or lay it flat?",
    "Hanging is usually better for long-term storage, because it stops flat spots forming on the buttocks, back and chest. Lying flat is fine for shorter periods if she's on a soft surface and you change her position regularly."
   ],
   [
    "Can I store my doll in its original box?",
    "For short periods, yes. Long-term box storage often causes flat spots and creases where the foam presses against the body. If she has to live in a box, take her out every few weeks to let her skin recover."
   ],
   [
    "Where should I keep my doll?",
    "Somewhere cool, dry and out of direct sunlight, such as a wardrobe or a spare room. Avoid lofts and garages, where temperatures swing and damp builds up, and keep her away from radiators."
   ],
   [
    "Why do my doll's joints feel loose?",
    "Some loosening is normal with use. Forcing poses, holding extreme positions for a long time and supporting her weight through one joint all speed it up. Many skeletons have screws that can be tightened. Check your manufacturer's guidance first."
   ],
   [
    "Can I leave my doll standing on standing feet?",
    "Only briefly, and only when she's supported. Standing bolts are for photos and dressing, not for storing her. If she falls unattended, the impact can damage the skeleton or tear the skin."
   ]
  ],
  "body": "Storage is the thing new owners worry about most, and the place most second-hand dolls pick up their damage. The good news is you don't need anything elaborate. You need a dry doll, a sensible position and the right place to keep her.\n\n## Before she goes anywhere: dry, powdered, undressed or lightly dressed\n\n- **Completely dry.** Any moisture left in internal areas can lead to mould. Use drying sticks until they come out dry (see our [cleaning guide](/guides/how-to-clean-a-pre-owned-doll)).\n- **Lightly powdered** if she's TPE, to stop the skin sticking to itself or to fabric.\n- **Undressed, or in white or light clothing.** Dark and cheap dyes transfer into TPE over time, and the marks are usually permanent.\n\n## Your storage options\n\n### 1. Hanging (the best option for most owners)\n\nA hanging kit lets her hang upright from a neck hook or a shoulder bolt. Nothing presses on her body, so her shape is preserved. That matters most for the bust, hips and buttocks, where flat spots form.\n\n- Use a kit designed for your doll's weight and skeleton, ideally from the manufacturer.\n- Make sure her feet don't take her weight against the floor.\n- Hang her somewhere she won't knock against a wall or door.\n\n### 2. Lying flat\n\nFine for shorter spells. Lay her on her back on a soft, padded surface such as a duvet or memory foam. **Don't** lay her on a hard floor or a wooden shelf, and change her position every week or two so pressure doesn't build up in one place.\n\n### 3. A storage case or box\n\nDiscreet, which is ideal if you share your space, but the most likely to cause pressure marks. If you use a case, make sure it's padded and long enough that she isn't bent, and take her out regularly.\n\n### 4. Head stands\n\nIf you store the head separately, a padded head stand keeps the face away from surfaces and protects the neck connection. Cover it loosely to keep dust off.\n\n## Looking after the skeleton\n\nMost modern dolls have a metal skeleton with articulated joints. A little loosening over time is normal, but you can slow it right down:\n\n- **Never force a joint.** If it resists, stop. Forcing past the natural range is how skin tears and joints fail.\n- **Don't hold extreme poses for long.** Arms above the head and legs spread wide put a lot of stress on joints and on the skin over them.\n- **Support her weight.** When lifting, support her under the back and knees rather than pulling by an arm or the neck.\n- **Rotate positions** so no single joint is always loaded.\n- **Use standing feet briefly and supervised.** They're for photos and dressing, not storage.\n\n## Where to keep her\n\nLook for somewhere **cool, dry and dark**:\n\n- ✅ A wardrobe, a spare room or a cupboard on an inside wall\n- ❌ Lofts and garages, where heat, cold and damp all cause problems\n- ❌ Next to radiators or in direct sunlight, which yellow and soften TPE\n- ❌ Touching another doll. Different materials, and different TPE skin tones, can mark each other.\n\n## If you're planning to sell\n\nHow she's been stored is one of the first things a buyer will ask about. \"Stored hanging in a cool, dry wardrobe since day one\" is worth real money in a listing. Include a photo of your hanging kit or stand if you can (see [how to list your doll so it sells](/guides/how-to-photograph-and-list-your-doll)).\n\n**Next:** [what is my used doll worth?](/guides/how-much-is-my-used-doll-worth)"
